{
  "sections": [
    {
      "heading": "Gustar works backwards",
      "body": "To say you like something, Spanish says \"it is pleasing to me\": *Me gusta el café* (literally: coffee pleases me). The thing you like is the subject, so the verb agrees with **it**, not with you:\n\n- One thing or an activity (infinitive): *gusta* — *Me gusta la música. Me gusta bailar.*\n- Several things: *gustan* — *Me gustan los perros.*\n\nThe person who likes is shown by a pronoun before the verb.",
      "table": {
        "head": [
          "Who likes",
          "Pronoun",
          "Example"
        ],
        "rows": [
          [
            "I",
            "*me*",
            "*Me gusta el chocolate.*"
          ],
          [
            "you (tú)",
            "*te*",
            "*¿Te gustan las películas?*"
          ],
          [
            "he / she / you (usted)",
            "*le*",
            "*Le gusta cocinar.*"
          ],
          [
            "we",
            "*nos*",
            "*Nos gusta viajar.*"
          ],
          [
            "you all / they",
            "*les*",
            "*Les gustan los gatos.*"
          ]
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "heading": "Making it clear and emphatic",
      "body": "*Le* and *les* can refer to several people, so you often add *a* + name/pronoun: *A María le gusta el té. A mis padres les gusta caminar.* For emphasis or contrast: *A mí me gusta, pero a ti no.* (I like it, but you don't.) — note *mí* has an accent, *ti* doesn't.\n\nNegative: *No me gusta el café.* To say you like something a lot: *Me gusta mucho*. For a strong \"love it\", use *encantar*, which works the same way: *Me encanta el helado.* (I love ice cream.) *Me encantan los libros.*"
    },
    {
      "heading": "Me too, me neither",
      "body": "Agreeing with someone uses *también* (too) and *tampoco* (neither):\n\n- *Me gusta el cine.* — *A mí también.* (Me too.)\n- *No me gusta el frío.* — *A mí tampoco.* (Me neither.)\n\nDisagreeing flips them: *Me gusta el cine.* — *A mí no.* (I don't.) *No me gusta el frío.* — *A mí sí.* (I do.)\n\nIn written dialogue Spanish marks each new speaker with a long dash (*—*) instead of quotation marks, as in the examples below."
    }
  ],
